From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niklas Sheth <hi@niklassheth.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 08:58:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: Per-submission timeline in a debugfs ring
 (timeline=1/2)

Debug parameter for attributing GPU idle at submission boundaries. When
set, each submission logs one line as its fence signals: the CNTVCT tick
at the ioctl, input fences still pending at the ioctl, microseconds from
the ioctl to the scheduler running the job, to its first and last
publication and to completion, and admission deferrals with their reasons
(backpressure bits; 0x100 a dependency hold). Off by default.

Folded in:
- WIP drm/asahi: G16 per-submission timeline (timeline=1)
- WIP drm/asahi: G16 timeline lines in microseconds, with pid and file id
- WIP drm/asahi: G16 timeline=2 splits completion and kick latency
- WIP drm/asahi: G16 timeline records into a debugfs binary ring

[WIP drm/asahi: G16 timeline lines in microseconds, with pid and file id]
CNTVCT runs at 1 GHz on T8132, not 24 MHz: #150's deltas were in 24 ns
units. Also name the client: pid, and a per-file id since every muvm guest
submits from the one krun process.

[WIP drm/asahi: G16 timeline=2 splits completion and kick latency]
timeline=2 adds, per submission: the doorbell after its first
publication, and for the completion message that retired it the IRQ
time (recv_message_early, stamp only), RTKit's workqueue delivery, the
pump's wake and the retire scan. With GPU timestamps on the same 1 GHz
counter this attributes GPU end -> fence signal and publish -> GPU start.

[WIP drm/asahi: G16 timeline records into a debugfs binary ring]
timeline=1/2 no longer printk one line per submission (~1.5k lines/s
under ER). Each retired submission is stored as a 160-byte record of
absolute CNTVCT ticks in a fixed ring (timeline_records, default
131072, allocated at probe only when timeline is set), read from
debugfs asahi_m4/timeline. The format is documented in g16_timeline.rs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
